Layer 2: System of Record, Authoritative Record, and Data Integrity Architecture
The Conceptual Hierarchy
In the DTCC ecosystem, the system of record is not a single database but a federated architecture of mutually-reinforcing authoritative sources:
DTC's CNS (Continuous Net Settlement) system: The authoritative record for securities positions and settlement obligations
NSCC's trade capture and clearance systems: Authoritative record for equity and fixed income trade matching and novation
FICC's GSD and MBSD systems: Authoritative record for government securities and mortgage-backed securities clearing
DDR (Data Repository): Authoritative record for swap and security-based swap transaction data under CFTC/SEC reporting mandates
Data Integrity Controls
DTCC employs a multi-layered data integrity architecture:
Validation Controls (Ingestion Layer)
Upon submission to systems like DDR, records undergo systemic validation checks:
Submitter must be recognized by the system
Submitter must be eligible to submit records
Submission must be in ingestible format
Submission must meet all Designated Regulator and system data format and content requirements
Rejected records are deemed not submitted with respect to the jurisdiction for which rejected
Consistency Controls (Processing Layer)
Net Debit Cap: Limits any Participant's net debit settlement obligation to the amount satisfiable with DTC liquidity resources
Collateral Monitor: Requires net debit settlement obligations to be fully collateralized as they accrue intraday
Position reconciliation: Participants are required to reconcile their DTC positions on a daily and month-end basis
Reconciliation Engine & Exception Management
Post-trade reconciliation operates across:
Counterparty confirmations
CCP data (DTC, NSCC, FICC)
Custodian records
Internal position systems
Under T+1 settlement (effective May 2024), the overnight buffer for break investigation was eliminated. Firms must now achieve same-day break detection. Exception management follows priority-based routing:
Break classification: quantity, price, settlement date, identifier mismatches
Investigation queue routing: based on break type and settlement deadline proximity
Escalation triggers: for breaks approaching settlement deadlines
Resolution tracking: full audit trail from detection to settlement confirmation
Data Lineage & Provenance
DTCC's data architecture tracks:
Technical metadata: schemas, data types, structural relationships
Business metadata: definitions, ownership, steward assignments
Operational metadata: refresh frequency, last update timestamps, query statistics
Governance metadata: sensitivity classifications, access restrictions, retention rules
For physical certificates, DTC maintains the Automated Inventory Management (AIM) system, which records each individual certificate at the certificate level. Vault areas are monitored by Global Security Management with badge and biometric access restriction.
Records Retention & Immutability
SEC Rule 17a-4 Compliance
DTCC and its participants are subject to SEC Rule 17a-4, which mandates:
WORM (Write Once, Read Many) storage format for electronic records
Retention periods: 3 years (communications, confirmations), 6 years (trade ledgers, general ledgers, customer account records), life of firm (organizational documents)
Indexing and retrieval: records must be retrievable within 24 hours of regulatory request
Third-party access: independent third party must be able to provide regulators direct access
DDR Specific Retention
Under DDR rules:
Swap and security-based swap records must be retrievable within 3 business days
In the event DDR ceases business, records must be preserved for not less than 5 years or until transfer to the Designated Regulator
Immutable Event Log Architecture
DTCC's systems maintain serialized, time-dated records with unique numeric entry IDs. For its Snowflake analytics infrastructure (deployed June 2020), DTCC utilizes:
Time Travel: query and retrieve deleted data for up to 90 days
Fail-safe: additional 7 days past retention period
Triple redundancy for all critical services
Automatic retries for failed query components
Causal Chain: Legislative retention mandate (17a-4) → WORM storage requirement → immutable event logging → data lineage tracking → validation at ingestion → consistency during processing → reconciliation at end-of-cycle → audit trail for regulators.
Layer 3: Clearing, Settlement, and Central Counterparty Mechanics
Novation and Multilateral Netting
When a trade is submitted for clearing:
Trade capture: NSCC/FICC captures trade details from executing venues
Validation: trade data validated against eligibility criteria
Novation: the CCP interposes itself as buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, becoming the counterparty to both sides
Netting: multilateral netting reduces gross obligations to net positions
Settlement: Delivery versus Payment (DvP) or Receive versus Payment (RvP) settlement occurs
Settlement Finality
DTC operates a DvP Model 2 DNS (Delivery versus Payment, Designated-Time Net Settlement) system. Settlement finality is achieved:
For Fedwire securities transfers: final and irrevocable when transfer posted per Federal Reserve Operating Circular 7
For funds-only settlement via NSS: final and irrevocable when posted per Federal Reserve Operating Circular 12
For clearing bank book-entry transfers: final when transfer occurs per agreement between FICC and its clearing bank (BNY Mellon)
Critical constraint: Members cannot revoke securities settlement obligations to FICC without express prior permission. Reversal codes are prohibited.
NSCC 24x5 Clearing Operations
As of June 29, 2026, NSCC extended clearing hours to 24x5 availability (Sundays 8:00 PM ET to Fridays 8:00 PM ET), supporting overnight trading from Alternative Trading Systems (ATS) and exchanges. This required:
Testing environment opened January 2026
All consumers of UTC real-time messages completed testing prior to go-live
Central counterparty guarantee applied immediately to extended-hours transactions
