Layer 4: Risk Management Framework (FRM / MRM / CFM)
Corporate Risk Framework
DTCC maintains a Corporate Risk Framework documented through the DTCC Corporate Risk Management Policy, which includes Risk Tolerance Statements for:
Clearance & Settlement Financial Risk
Operational Risk (including Data Management Risk, Model Risk, Legal & Regulatory Compliance Risk, Processing & Operations Risk, IT Risk, Cybersecurity & Information Protection Risk, Human Capital Risk, Business Continuity Risk, Third Party Risk)
Corporate Financial Risk
Strategic Risk
Each Risk Tolerance Statement identifies:
Applicable Risk Families
Key indicators/metrics categorizing tolerance thresholds ("within tolerance" to "exceeds tolerance")
Governance and escalation protocols for breaches
The Corporate Risk Management Policy is reviewed, updated, and approved by the MRC at least annually, and reviewed and approved by the BRC annually.
Model Risk Management (MRM)
DTCC and its affiliates have adopted the Clearing Agency Model Risk Management Framework, filed with and approved by the SEC, reviewed and approved by the Board annually.
MRM responsibilities:
Maintains model inventory of all methods and systems meeting DTCC's definition of a model
Validates all models and material changes prior to production use
Reviews margin stress-testing and CRRM models at least annually
Escalates deficiencies to MRC and BRC
Model validation scope:
Clearing Fund formula models
Stress testing models
All new models subject to independent validation and approval/provisional approval by MRM before production use
Financial Risk Management (FRM)
FRM operates within the Group Chief Risk Office and manages:
Credit risk
Market risk
Liquidity risk
Margin Methodology
VaR-based: 10-year lookback period with additional stress period if historical lookback inadequate
VaR Floor / Minimum Margin Amount: addresses procyclicality
Backtesting: conducted daily on individual member portfolios; exceptions reviewed by FRM management daily; reported to MRC monthly; shared with BRC and supervisors
Members with repeated deficiencies may be contacted and Backtesting Charges imposed
Liquidity Risk Management
Managed by the Liquidity Risk Management (LRM) team within FRM, subject to MRC and BRC oversight.
DTC Liquidity Resources (qualifying liquid resources per 17ad-22(a)(14)):
Participants Fund: at least $1.15 billion
Committed line of credit: $1.9 billion
Cash proceeds from DTC Senior Unsecured Notes
FICC Liquidity Framework:
Cover One Requirement: measures sufficiency to settle outstanding transactions of defaulting member with largest aggregate liquidity exposure in extreme but plausible conditions
FICC settles only in U.S. dollars
Daily liquidity studies across range of foreseeable scenarios
NSCC Liquidity:
NSCC uses SLD (Supplemental Liquidity Deposit), a payment obligation determined when daily liquidity obligation exceeds qualifying liquid resources (QLR) after stressed market assumptions
NSCC held SLD on 4 days in Q1 2026
QLR includes: cash deposits to Clearing Fund, proceeds of commercial paper/extendable notes/term debt, cash from committed 364-day credit facility
Clearing Fund / Participants Fund
The Clearing Fund (NSCC/FICC) and Participants Fund (DTC) serve as the primary prefunded financial resources.
FICC Clearing Fund:
Calculated using VaR methodology
Backtested daily
Members may pledge Eligible Clearing Fund Securities
DTC Participants Fund:
Minimum $1.15 billion
Used to cover potential losses from Participant default
Collateralized via Collateral Monitor and constrained by Net Debit Cap
Causal Chain: Risk appetite (BRC) → Risk tolerance statements (MRC) → Model validation (MRM) → Margin calculation (FRM) → Backtesting → Exception monitoring → Escalation to MRC/BRC → Capital adequacy verification.
Layer 5: Security Information & Event Management (SIEM), Access Control, and Non-Repudiation
Cybersecurity Framework Requirements
DTCC requires all Members/Participants to demonstrate use of an industry-accepted cyber risk management framework. Accepted frameworks include:
CRI Profile (Cyber Risk Institute Profile, formerly FSSCC Profile)
NIST CSF (National Institute of Standards and Technology Cybersecurity Framework)
ISO 27001/27002
FFIEC CAT
CSC 20 (Critical Security Controls Top 20)
SOC 2
SOC for Cybersecurity
COBIT
OSFI Cyber Security Self-Assessment Guidance
JASDEC Basic Policy on Information Security
FINRA Small Firm Cybersecurity Checklist
SEC OCIE Cybersecurity Examination Initiative
Control Officer Requirement
Each member firm must designate a Control Officer—a senior executive responsible and accountable for overseeing the cybersecurity program. This is typically the CISO, a Board member, or Security Manager. The Control Officer must complete a Confirmation Form attesting that the firm has a written cybersecurity program structured from an industry-known framework, subject to periodic review and update.
Security Operations Architecture
While DTCC does not publicly disclose its internal SIEM vendor stack, the operational requirements for a SIFMU-grade SOC include:
SIEM (Security Information and Event Management)
Centralized log aggregation from all critical systems (CNS, GSD, MBSD, CMU, Risk Management, Trade Capture)
Real-time event correlation across the settlement infrastructure
Long-term log retention aligned with SEC 17a-4 requirements
Integration with threat intelligence platforms
IDS/IPS (Intrusion Detection/Prevention Systems)
Network traffic monitoring at all ingress/egress points
Signature-based and behavioral anomaly detection
Automated blocking of confirmed malicious traffic
EDR/XDR (Endpoint/Extended Detection and Response)
Deep visibility into endpoint activities across all production servers
Cross-domain detection extending to cloud workloads and network devices
Automated isolation of compromised endpoints
UEBA (User and Entity Behavior Analytics)
Baseline establishment for normal user and system activity
ML-driven deviation detection for insider threats and compromised credentials
Contextual risk scoring for access anomalies
PAM (Privileged Access Management)
Password vaulting and rotation for privileged accounts
Session isolation and monitoring
Just-in-time access provisioning
Full session recording for forensic analysis
Access Control & Authentication
DTCC's access control framework operates across multiple layers:
Identity Management (IAM): lifecycle management for all user identities across the organization
Multi-factor authentication (MFA): required for all critical system access
Least privilege: access rights limited to minimum necessary for role
Role-based access control (RBAC): permissions mapped to organizational roles
Non-repudiation: all settlement instructions and critical transactions cryptographically attributable to the originating party
Cyber Incident Response & Disconnection Protocol
DTCC maintains a formal disconnect/reconnect protocol for member firms experiencing cybersecurity incidents:
Disconnection triggers:
Major system event affecting integrity or availability of data
Inability to make informed risk assessment due to lack of information from member
Authorization required from at least two voting members of DTCC Executive Committee
Reconnection requirements:
Restoration of affected networks to known trusted state
Demonstrated integrity of remediated systems, data, and components
Third-party assessment verifying:
Industry best practice protections deployed, tested, in place
Patching updated across systems
Additional cybersecurity controls implemented
Enhanced monitoring enabled
Written attestation from senior leadership confirming containment and elimination of threats
Assurance and integrity tests performed on services, applications, systems, backup systems, and network
Causal Chain: Legislative cyber mandate (Dodd-Frank/SEC) → Framework adoption (NIST CSF/ISO27001) → Control Officer designation → SIEM monitoring → Anomaly detection → Incident escalation → Executive Committee authorization → Disconnection → Forensic remediation → Third-party validation → Reconnection attestation → Service restoration.
