Layer 7: Default Management, Loss Allocation, and Recovery & Wind-Down
Default Management Process (DMP)
When a member defaults, the CCP activates its Default Management Process with the objective of re-establishing a matched book, primarily through auction of the defaulted member's portfolio to non-defaulting members.
Loss Allocation Waterfall
The loss waterfall operates in strict sequence:
Step 1: Defaulting Member's Resources
Amounts on deposit to the Clearing Fund / Participants Fund
Eligible Clearing Fund Securities pledged
Any other resources of or attributable to the defaulting member accessible under Rules
Step 2: Cross-Guaranty Agreements
Multilateral Agreement (DTC, NSCC, FICC, OCC, dated January 1, 2003, as amended): sharing of defaulting member's excess assets across clearing agencies
If NSCC has extra collateral for a defaulting member after close-out, FICC can use excess to cover shortfalls (and vice versa)
Step 3: DTCC Corporate Contribution (Skin-in-the-Game)
DTC applies 50% of its General Business Risk Capital Requirement as of the end of the calendar quarter immediately preceding the Event Period
For subsequent events within 250 days, contribution reduced to remaining unused portion
Step 4: Non-Defaulting Members' Clearing Fund / Participants Fund
Remaining loss allocated among non-defaulting members ratably in accordance with their required deposit to the fund on the first day of the period
Each member must pay within two business days of notice
DTC has right to debit allocated amount to member's Settlement Account
Step 5: Assessment Powers / Cash Calls
Limited, pre-defined cash assessments to solvent members
Linked to their share in pre-funded default fund or aggregate activity level
Step 6: Loss Allocation Measures (Extreme)
PRO (Pro-Rata Reduction in Unpaid Payment Obligations): variation margin haircutting
Partial/Full Tear-ups: termination of open positions to return to matched book
Historical note: Neither DTC, NSCC, nor FICC has ever implemented its loss allocation waterfall. Neither subsidiary has ever had to tap liquidity facilities, despite managing multiple defaults in DTCC's history.
Recovery & Wind-Down (R&W) Plans
Each clearing agency maintains a Recovery and Wind-down Plan, reviewed and approved by the Board at least every 12 months or following material changes.
R&W Plan components:
Recovery Plan: tools and strategies to recover from losses exceeding prefunded resources
Wind-down Plan: tools and strategies for orderly wind-down and transfer of business if recovery fails
Recovery tools available:
Identifying, monitoring, and managing general business risk
Holding sufficient liquid net assets funded by equity (LNA) per Capital Policy
Maintaining Capital Replenishment Plan (filed and approved by SEC)
Loss allocation process per Rule 4
Governance:
Office of Recovery & Resolution Planning (R&R Team): develops and maintains R&W Plans
Executive Committee: oversees Recovery & Resolution Planning Program
Board: reviews and approves R&W Plan annually
Facilitating Rules:
DTC: Rule 32A (Wind-down), Rule 38 (Market Disruption and Force Majeure), Rule 4 (Participants Fund and Loss Allocation)
NSCC: Rule 42 (Wind-down), Rule 41 (Corporation Default), Rule 60 (Market Disruption and Force Majeure), Rule 4 (Clearing Fund)
FICC: GSD Rule 22D, MBSD Rule 17B (Wind-down), GSD Rule 50, MBSD Rule 40 (Market Disruption and Force Majeure), GSD/MBSD Rule 4 (Clearing Fund and Loss Allocation)
CCLF (Committed Credit Line Facility) — FICC Specific
FICC maintains the Committed Credit Line Facility as a liquidity tool:
Once invoked, solvent members receive report identifying delivery obligations owed to defaulting member
Solvent members hold and fund deliveries up to maximum contribution amount
Members suppress securities deliveries and set up reverse repos against FICC
For MBSD: repos remain open until liquidation of underlying securities
For GSD: repos remain open until 30 calendar days (U.S. government bonds) or 60 calendar days (MBS) after CCLF entry
FICC reimburses members for financing costs (becomes part of liquidation P&L)
Causal Chain: Member default detected → DMP activated → portfolio auction → defaulting member resources exhausted → cross-guaranty applied → DTCC corporate capital applied → non-defaulting member funds allocated → cash calls if needed → PRO/tear-ups as last resort → if all fail → R&W Plan execution → wind-down and business transfer.
