Conclusion: Deterministic Interdependence
The U.S. securities clearing infrastructure is not a collection of independent subsystems. It is a causally chained deterministic architecture where:
Legislative authority (Congress) authorizes regulatory standards (SEC Rule 17ad-22)
Regulatory standards mandate governance structures (Board, BRC, MRC, Three Lines of Defense)
Governance structures enforce risk management frameworks (Corporate Risk Policy, Risk Tolerance Statements, Model Risk Framework)
Risk frameworks size financial resources (Clearing Fund, Participants Fund, liquidity facilities)
Financial resources protect settlement finality (DvP, RvP, Fedwire, NSS)
Settlement finality depends on data integrity (system of record, immutable logs, reconciliation, exception management)
Data integrity depends on cybersecurity controls (SIEM, IAM, PAM, MFA, SOC)
Cybersecurity depends on operational resilience (BCP, DR, failover, RTO/RPO)
Operational resilience ensures continuity of clearing services even under member default or systemic stress
Default management and recovery & wind-down plans provide the terminal backstop before resolution authority activation
A breach, failure, or compromise at any single layer propagates with mathematical certainty to all downstream layers. The architecture is designed not to prevent failure at any one point, but to ensure that no single point of failure can cascade through the entire chain without being intercepted by a control, a resource buffer, or a governance escalation protocol.
This is the deterministic reality of U.S. securities market infrastructure in 2026.
